o;;0;[o;;[I"uAdd the loaded box on iseq to check if another box tries running the iseq (add a field only when VM_CHECK_MODE?);To;;0;[o;;[I"-Assign its own TOPLEVEL_BINDING in boxes;To;;0;[o;;[I"sFix calling <code>warn</code> in boxes to refer <code>$VERBOSE</code> and <code>Warning.warn</code> in the box;To;;0;[o;;[I"RMake an internal data container class <code>Ruby::Box::Entry</code> invisible;To;;0;[o;;[I"TMore test cases about <code>$LOAD_PATH</code> and <code>$LOADED_FEATURES</code>;To;	;
i;I"How to use;To;	;
i;I"Enabling Ruby Box;To;;[I""First, an environment variable should be set at the ruby process bootup: <code>RUBY_BOX=1</code>.
The only valid value is <code>1</code> to enable Ruby Box. Other values (or unset <code>RUBY_BOX</code>) means disabling Ruby Box. And setting the value after Ruby program starts doesn't work.;To;	;
i;I"Using Ruby Box;To;;[I"@<code>Ruby::Box</code> class is the entrypoint of Ruby Box.;To:RDoc::Markup::Verbatim;[I"Nbox = Ruby::Box.new
box.require('something') # or require_relative, load
;T:@format:	rubyo;;[I"ΩThe required file (either .rb or .so/.dll/.bundle) is loaded in the box (<code>box</code> here). The required/loaded files from <code>something</code> will be loaded in the box recursively.;To;;[I"O# something.rb

X = 1

class Something
  def self.x = X
  def x = ::X
end
;T;;o;;[I"qClasses/modules, those methods and constants defined in the box can be accessed via <code>box</code> object.;To;;[I"kX = 2
p X                 # 2
p ::X               # 2
p box::Something.x  # 1
p box::X            # 1
;T;;o;;[I"NInstance methods defined in the box also run with definitions in the box.;To;;[I"(s = box::Something.new

p s.x  # 1
;T;;o;	;
i;I"Specifications;To;	;
i;I"Ruby Box types;To;;[I"There are three box types:;To;;;;[o;;0;[o;;[I"Master box;To;;0;[o;;[I"Root box;To;;0;[o;;[I"User boxes;To;;[I"/Ruby bootstrap runs in the root box, and a;To;;[I"°There is the root box, just a single box in a Ruby process. All builtin classes/modules are defined and run in the root box. (See "Builtin classes and modules".);To;;[I"oUser boxes are to run user-written programs and libraries loaded from user programs. The user's main program (specified by the <code>ruby</code> command line argument) is executed in the "main" box, which is a user box automatically created at the end of Ruby's bootstrap. The files specified with <code>-r</code> command line option will be required in the main box.;To;;[I"|Calling <code>Ruby::Box.new</code> creates an "optional" box (a user, non-main box), technically equal to the main box.;To;;[I"€Ruby also has the master box. The master box is the "master copy" of all boxes. Boxes will be created as a copy of the master box. The master box is only for the source of box copies, and no code runs in the master box.;To;;[I"_[master]
 |
 |----[root]
 |
 |----[main]
 |
 |----[user box 1]
 |
 |----[user box 2]
 ...
;T;0o;	;
i;I"!Ruby Box class and instances;To;;[I"å<code>Ruby::Box</code> is a class, as a subclass of <code>Module</code>. <code>Ruby::Box</code> instances are a kind of <code>Module</code>.;To;	;
i;I")Classes and modules defined in boxes;To;;[I"ÓThe classes and modules, newly defined in a box <code>box</code>, are accessible via <code>box</code>. For example, if a class <code>A</code> is defined in <code>box</code>, it is accessible as <code>box::A</code> from outside of the box.;To;;[I"mIn the box <code>box</code>, <code>A</code> can be referred to as <code>A</code> (and <code>::A</code>).;To;	;
i;I"3Built-in classes and modules reopened in boxes;To;;[I"ŒIn boxes, builtin classes/modules are visible and can be reopened. Those classes/modules can be reopened using <code>class</code> or <code>module</code> clauses, and class/module definitions can be changed.;To;;[I"íThe changed definitions are visible only in the box. In other boxes, builtin classes/modules and those instances work without changed definitions.;To;;[I"©# in foo.rb
class String
  BLANK_PATTERN = /\A\s*\z/
  def blank?
    self =~ BLANK_PATTERN
  end
end

module Foo
  def self.foo = "foo"

  def self.foo_is_blank?
    foo.blank?
  end
end

Foo.foo.blank? #=> false
"foo".blank?   #=> false

# in main.rb
box = Ruby::Box.new
box.require('foo')

box::Foo.foo_is_blank? #=> false   (#blank? called in box)

"foo".blank?          # NoMethodError
String::BLANK_PATTERN # NameError
;T;;o;;[I"~The main box and <code>box</code> above are different boxes, so monkey patches in main are also invisible in <code>box</code>.;To;	;
i;I" Builtin classes and modules;To;;[I"OIn the box context, "builtin" classes and modules are classes and modules:;To;;;;[o;;0;[o;;[I"FAccessible without any <code>require</code> calls in user scripts;To;;0;[o;;[I"2Defined before any user program start running;To;;[I"\Hereafter, "builtin classes and modules" will be referred to as just "builtin classes".;To;;[I"RBuiltin classes and modules are loaded in all boxes, and run in the root box.;To;	;
i;I"-Exceptional non-built-in classes/modules;To;;[I"There are some exceptional classes/modules that are enabled in default, but aren't built-in classes. Those classes/modules are:;To;;;;[	o;;0;[o;;[I"<code>RubyGems</code>;To;;0;[o;;[I" <code>ErrorHighlight</code>;To;;0;[o;;[I"<code>DidYouMean</code>;To;;0;[o;;[I"<code>SyntaxSuggest</code>;To;;[I"≈Those classes/modules (part of default gems) are loaded in each boxes independently. If a user box's code calls RubyGems, it calls the RubyGems inside the box itself, instead of the root box's one.;To;	;
i;I"-Builtin classes referred via box objects;To;;[I"(Builtin classes in a box <code>box</code> can be referred from other boxes. For example, <code>box::String</code> is a valid reference, and <code>String</code> and <code>box::String</code> are identical (<code>String == box::String</code>, <code>String.object_id == box::String.object_id</code>).;To;;[I"¨<code>box::String</code>-like reference returns just a <code>String</code> in the current box, so its definition is <code>String</code> in the box, not in <code>box</code>.;To;;[I"Ö# foo.rb
class String
  def self.foo = "foo"
end

# main.rb
box = Ruby::Box.new
box.require('foo')

box::String.foo  # NoMethodError
;T;;o;	;
i;I"9Class instance variables, class variables, constants;To;;[I"vBuiltin classes can have different sets of class instance variables, class variables and constants between boxes.;To;;[I"|# foo.rb
class Array
  @v = "foo"
  @@v = "_foo_"
  V = "FOO"
end

Array.instance_variable_get(:@v) #=> "foo"
Array.class_variable_get(:@@v)   #=> "_foo_"
Array.const_get(:V)              #=> "FOO"

# main.rb
box = Ruby::Box.new
box.require('foo')

Array.instance_variable_get(:@v) #=> nil
Array.class_variable_get(:@@v)   # NameError
Array.const_get(:V)              # NameError
;T;;o;	;
i;I"Global variables;To;;[I"èIn boxes, changes on global variables are also isolated in the boxes. Changes on global variables in a box are visible/applied only in the box.;To;;[I"Ê# foo.rb
$foo = "foo"
$VERBOSE = nil

puts "This appears: '#{$foo}'"

# main.rb
p $foo      #=> nil
p $VERBOSE  #=> false

box = Ruby::Box.new
box.require('foo')  # "This appears: 'foo'"

p $foo      #=> nil
p $VERBOSE  #=> false
;T;;o;	;
i;I"Top level constants;To;;[I"Usually, top level constants are defined as constants of <code>Object</code>. In boxes, top level constants are constants of <code>Object</code> in the box. And the box object <code>box</code>'s constants are strictly equal to constants of <code>Object</code>.;To;;[I"∏# foo.rb
FOO = 100

FOO         #=> 100
Object::FOO #=> 100

# main.rb
box = Ruby::Box.new
box.require('foo')

box::FOO      #=> 100

FOO          # NameError
Object::FOO  # NameError
;T;;o;	;
i;I"Top level methods;To;;[I"XTop level methods are private instance methods of <code>Object</code>, in each box.;To;;[I"√# foo.rb
def yay = "foo"

class Foo
  def self.say = yay
end

Foo.say #=> "foo"
yay     #=> "foo"

# main.rb
box = Ruby::Box.new
box.require('foo')

box::Foo.say  #=> "foo"

yay  # NoMethodError
;T;;o;;[I"ùThere is no way to expose top level methods in boxes to others.
(See "Expose top level methods as a method of the box object" in "Discussions" section below);To;	;
i;I"Ruby Box scopes;To;;[I"RRuby Box works in file scope. One <code>.rb</code> file runs in a single box.;To;;[I"|Once a file is loaded in a box <code>box</code>, all methods/procs defined/created in the file run in <code>box</code>.;To;	;
i;I"Utility methods;To;;[I"?Several methods are available for trying/testing Ruby Box.;To;;;;[
o;;0;[o;;[I";<code>Ruby::Box.current</code> returns the current box;To;;0;[o;;[I"p<code>Ruby::Box.enabled?</code> returns true/false to represent <code>RUBY_BOX=1</code> is specified or not;To;;0;[o;;[I"5<code>Ruby::Box.root</code> returns the root box;To;;0;[o;;[I"5<code>Ruby::Box.main</code> returns the main box;To;;0;[o;;[I"Ä<code>Ruby::Box#eval</code> evaluates a Ruby code (String) in the receiver box, just like calling <code>#load</code> with a file;To;	;
i;I"Implementation details;To;	;
i	;I"&ISeq inline method/constant cache;To;;[I"áAs described above in "Ruby Box scopes", an ".rb" file runs in a box. So method/constant resolution will be done in a box consistently.;To;;[I"TThat means ISeq inline caches work well even with boxes. Otherwise, it's a bug.;To;	;
i	;I"%Method call global cache (gccct);To;;[I"á<code>rb_funcall()</code> C function refers to the global cc cache table (gccct), and the cache key is calculated with the current box.;To;;[I"]So, <code>rb_funcall()</code> calls have a performance penalty when Ruby Box is enabled.;To;	;
i	;I" Current box and loading box;To;;[I"}The current box is the box that the executing code is in. <code>Ruby::Box.current</code> returns the current box object.;To;;[I"«The loading box is an internally managed box to determine the box to load newly required/loaded files. For example, <code>box</code> is the loading box when <code>box.require("foo")</code> is called.;To;	;
i;I"Discussions;To;	;
i	;I")More builtin methods written in Ruby;To;;[I"ÕIf Ruby Box is enabled by default, builtin methods can be written in Ruby because it can't be overridden by users' monkey patches. Builtin Ruby methods can be JIT-ed, and it could bring performance reward.;To;	;
i	;I"6Monkey patching methods called by builtin methods;To;;[I"Builtin methods sometimes call other builtin methods. For example, <code>Hash#map</code> calls <code>Hash#each</code> to retrieve entries to be mapped. Without Ruby Box, Ruby users can overwrite <code>Hash#each</code> and expect the behavior change of <code>Hash#map</code> as a result.;To;;[I"=But with boxes, <code>Hash#map</code> runs in the root box. Ruby users can define <code>Hash#each</code> only in user boxes, so users cannot change <code>Hash#map</code>'s behavior in this case. To achieve it, users should override both<code>Hash#map</code> and <code>Hash#each</code> (or only <code>Hash#map</code>).;To;;[I"It is a breaking change.;To;;[I"jUsers can define methods using <code>Ruby::Box.root.eval(...)</code>, but it's clearly not ideal API.;To;	;
i	;I"AAssigning values to global variables used by builtin methods;To;;[I"¬Similar to monkey patching methods, global variables assigned in a box is separated from the root box. Methods defined in the root box referring a global variable can't find the re-assigned one.;To;	;
i	;I"IContext of <code>$LOAD_PATH</code> and <code>$LOADED_FEATURES</code>;To;;[I" Global variables <code>$LOAD_PATH</code> and <code>$LOADED_FEATURES</code> control <code>require</code> method behaviors. So those variables are determined by the loading box instead of the current box.;To;;[I"_This could potentially conflict with the user's expectations. We should find the solution.;To;	;
i	;I";Expose top level methods as a method of the box object;To;;[I"ñCurrently, top level methods in boxes are not accessible from outside of the box. But there might be a use case to call other box's top level methods.;To;	;
i	;I"mSeparate <code>cc_tbl</code> and <code>callable_m_tbl</code>, <code>cvc_tbl</code> for less classext CoW;To;;[I"ÏThe fields of <code>rb_classext_t</code> contains several cache(-like) data, <code>cc_tbl</code>(callcache table), <code>callable_m_tbl</code>(table of resolved complemented methods) and <code>cvc_tbl</code>(class variable cache table).;To;;[I"aThe classext CoW is triggered when the contents of <code>rb_classext_t</code> are changed, including <code>cc_tbl</code>, <code>callable_m_tbl</code>, and <code>cvc_tbl</code>. But those three tables are changed by just calling methods or referring class variables. So, currently, classext CoW is triggered much more times than the original expectation.;To;;[I"´If we can move those three tables outside of <code>rb_classext_t</code>, the number of copied <code>rb_classext_t</code> will be much less than the current implementation.;T:
